---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Motif from "../components/Motif.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import ns from "../data/namesakes.json";
const sets = ns.sets;
const total = sets.reduce((n, s) => n + s.who.length, 0);
const motifs = ["koppie", "windpump", "gable", "wagon", "awl", "thorn", "koppie"];
---
<Base title="The namesakes"
      eyebrow={`${sets.length} names, ${total} people`}
      dek="In this family a forename is inherited property. It travels through daughters as reliably as a surname travels through sons, it survives a change of surname, and it goes on being given long after the person it came from is dead.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why this is not decoration</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>{ns.note}</p>
    <blockquote>Read these lists and the family's own idea of itself is visible in a way no document
      states: who was worth remembering, whose side of the family was being honoured, and which
      relationships mattered enough to be carried for a century.</blockquote>
    <p>The first list is the strangest. <b>The name this family has repeated for five generations
      belongs to a man with no blood relation to any of them.</b></p>
  </div>
</section>

{sets.map((s, i) => (
  <div>
    <Rule name={motifs[i % motifs.length]} />
    <section class="blk">
      <div class="lab">{s.sub}</div>
      <h2 style="margin-top:6px">{s.n}</h2>
      <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">{s.kick}</p>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th style="width:21rem">Who</th><th class="m" style="width:9rem">When</th><th>What they are to the name</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {s.who.map(([n, when, why, href]) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{href ? <a href={u(href)}><strong>{n}</strong></a> : <strong>{n}</strong>}</td>
                <td class="m">{when}</td>
                <td>{why}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      {s.note && <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px">{s.note}</p>}
    </section>
  </div>
))}

<Rule name="thorn" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The same habit that makes this family legible makes it dangerous to index.
      <b>Three of this archive's corrections are name collisions</b>, and one of them stood for
      weeks: an estate filed in 1885 was given to the wrong Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus, and a death date
      was invented to fit.</p>
    <p>So this site does not merge people on a name. Where two people share one, they share a page and
      the page says so out loud; where a name is carried by more than one person, every chart and every
      register row keeps them apart.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was taken back &rarr;</a> ·
      <a href={u("/people/")}>Everyone &rarr;</a> ·
      <a href={u("/name/")}>The surname &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
